WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.520
About five hundred human kinases hold ATP in nearly the same pocket.

2
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.820
How can one small drug shut down just one kinase?

3
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.000
And what can you do when a tumour fights back?

4
00:00:15.983 --> 00:00:21.603
Every protein kinase moves the end phosphate of ATP onto another protein.

5
00:00:22.843 --> 00:00:27.743
To run that transfer, active kinases fold their pockets into nearly the same shape.

6
00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:32.603
That is why one pocket's drug was expected to jam dozens.

7
00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:36.247
Imatinib broke that rule in two ways.

8
00:00:37.607 --> 00:00:41.807
First, working shapes must match, yet switched-off shapes are thought to differ

9
00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:48.407
between kinases. Imatinib fits an idle shape that few kinases adopt the same way.

10
00:00:49.587 --> 00:00:54.127
So it hits a short list: ABL, the PDGF receptor, and KIT.

11
00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.210
Still, normal cells use those same three kinases.

12
00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:04.150
So the second layer is target dependence: how much a cell relies on the blocked

13
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:12.570
kinase. Chronic myeloid leukaemia cells carry a fused, always-on kinase, BCR-ABL,

14
00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:13.990
and cannot live without it.

15
00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:19.030
Normal cells tolerate a pause, though tissue injury can still occur.

16
00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:21.613
Then came a sharp turn.

17
00:01:22.873 --> 00:01:25.993
Some patients who improved on imatinib later relapsed.

18
00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:33.573
Sequencing BCR-ABL from their blood revealed a single changed residue at position

19
00:01:33.573 --> 00:01:40.653
315. Threonine had turned into isoleucine, a bulkier side chain sitting beside the

20
00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:47.847
Position 315 is called the gatekeeper residue, because it guards a back alcove

21
00:01:47.847 --> 00:01:49.587
beside the ATP site.

22
00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:54.927
Imatinib reaches inside and makes a hydrogen bond with threonine.

23
00:01:56.367 --> 00:02:00.267
Bulkier isoleucine cannot make that bond, and it blocks imatinib.

24
00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:05.327
Yet ATP never uses the alcove, so the kinase keeps firing.

25
00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:10.167
A relapse feels like failure, yet it works like an experiment.

26
00:02:11.587 --> 00:02:17.167
Changing one drug contact, with catalysis intact, is strong evidence that imatinib

27
00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:18.167
hit the right target.

28
00:02:19.407 --> 00:02:21.167
The tumour still needed that kinase.

29
00:02:22.627 --> 00:02:23.627
Pause and predict.

30
00:02:24.687 --> 00:02:28.647
To beat this mutant, how must the next drug change at position 315?

31
00:02:35.017 --> 00:02:40.957
Because imatinib's hold depends on threonine 315, the next drug had to grip without

32
00:02:40.957 --> 00:02:47.497
that contact. So chemists gave ponatinib a slim, straight carbon-carbon triple bond

33
00:02:47.497 --> 00:02:49.757
right where isoleucine sticks out.

34
00:02:51.017 --> 00:02:55.977
That slender bridge clears the bulky side chain, so ponatinib shuts down the mutant

35
00:02:55.977 --> 00:02:56.977
kinase.

36
00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.940
Hold two principles as you read on.

37
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:06.460
Where a drug binds shapes its selectivity, alongside how much the tumour relies on

38
00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:12.400
that target. And when resistance alters a drug contact, reading that escape guides

39
00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:13.180
the next drug.
